C = [
("h1", "12. What the Volume Does Not Say"),
("p", "Claim: a volume that argues a position is bound to say where the position stops. The ruling governs the programs and the gifts the text describes. It does not govern what the text does not state, and the volume lists what it leaves unsaid so that no reader supposes it has said more."),
("sub", "12.1 Nothing about God’s power"),
("p", "The ruling makes no statement about God’s power. It does not say that God cannot speak, heal or act by His Spirit in any person at any time. It says that the sign-program associated with Israel has ceased and that its gifts do not continue into the Mystery Program. A believer may pray, and God is sovereign in mercy. The volume does not argue about what God does. It argues about what the text describes as a gift and a program."),
("sub", "12.2 Nothing about the meaning of that which is perfect"),
("p", "The volume reports 1 Corinthians 13:10 as written. It does not say that the perfect is the return of Christ, the completed canon, the maturity of the church or anything else. It holds that the ruling stands whichever of these is chosen, and a reader who holds one of the readings can follow the argument."),
("sub", "12.3 Nothing about the order of the verbs"),
("p", "The difference in the Greek verbs of 13:8 is reported and no argument is built on it. The sentence says that prophecies shall fail, tongues shall cease and knowledge shall vanish away, and it does not give an order."),
("sub", "12.4 Nothing from the italic unknown"),
("p", "The word “unknown” in 1 Corinthians 14 is the translators’ word, printed in italic. The volume builds nothing on it. The comparison to the kinds of voices in the world in 14:10, 11 stands on the text of Paul, and the volume reads it for itself."),
("sub", "12.5 Nothing about the Spirit’s other work"),
("p", "The gifts of 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 are not the whole of the Spirit’s work. The Spirit baptizes into one body (1 Corinthians 12:13), and his work of sealing, indwelling and leading is treated in the studies that read those passages. The volume says nothing here about those works, because the passages it reads do not touch them."),
("sub", "12.6 Nothing about the offices"),
("p", "The offices of apostle and prophet belong to Volume 4. This volume has not decided whether the prophets of Ephesians 3:5 and 4:11 are the same as the speakers of 1 Corinthians 14, and it does not say."),
("sub", "12.7 Nothing about another church’s practice"),
("p", "The volume describes no church, movement or person. It reads Scripture. A reader who knows a practice that is called by the name of tongues or prophecy can ask of it the questions the volume asks of the text: what is it, to whom is it a sign, and what program does it belong to. The volume leaves the application to the reader."),
("ctbl", "What the volume says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("Question", "The volume says", "The volume leaves unsaid"),
    ("What are tongues?", "Languages, a sign to them that believe not", "Whether any person has spoken them since Acts 28"),
    ("What is prophecy?", "A revealed word to edify, exhort and comfort", "The meaning of the prophets in Ephesians"),
    ("When do the gifts end?", "With the sign-program at Acts 28", "A date for the perfect of 1 Corinthians 13:10"),
    ("What of God’s power?", "Nothing; the ruling governs the programs", "What God does in any person"),
  ], [0.25, 0.38, 0.37]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The ruling governs the programs and makes no statement about God’s power.",
    "The perfect, the order of the verbs, the italic word and the offices of Ephesians are left where the text leaves them.",
    "The volume describes no church or movement; it reads Scripture.",
  ]),
]
